# Line endings.
#
# The project is developed on Windows and deployed on Linux. Without this file Git
# converts everything to CRLF in the working tree, and the shell scripts and the systemd
# unit then fail on the target with `bash: $'\r': command not found` — a failure whose
# message points nowhere near its cause.
#
# LF everywhere is the simple, correct answer: Rust, shell, TOML and Markdown all read
# LF fine on Windows, and every editor in use here handles it.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Anything that is executed on a Linux host must be LF, without exception.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.service     text eol=lf
install.sh    text eol=lf

# NSIS reads its script with the Windows conventions; leave this one alone.
*.nsi         text eol=crlf

# Binary, so Git neither converts nor diffs them.
*.png         binary
*.jpg         binary
*.ico         binary
*.icns        binary
*.der         binary
*.keystore    binary
*.jks         binary

# Generated, and large: keep it out of diffs and merge conflicts.
Cargo.lock    -diff

# GitHub's language statistics should describe the code, not the vendored fixtures.
docs/**       linguist-documentation
